1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,734
Day four.

2
00:00:01,734 --> 00:00:02,927
No sleep.

3
00:00:02,927 --> 00:00:04,216
And there's someone standing in

4
00:00:04,216 --> 00:00:05,824
the corner of your room.

5
00:00:05,925 --> 00:00:07,118
You blink...

6
00:00:07,118 --> 00:00:08,439
and they're gone.

7
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:09,982
This is what happens when

8
00:00:09,982 --> 00:00:11,211
you try to stay awake

9
00:00:11,211 --> 00:00:12,685
for eleven days.

10
00:00:12,685 --> 00:00:14,185
And a real teenager actually

11
00:00:14,185 --> 00:00:15,206
did it.

12
00:00:15,807 --> 00:00:16,873
What if you stayed awake

13
00:00:16,873 --> 00:00:18,536
for eleven days straight?

14
00:00:20,426 --> 00:00:21,198
San Diego,

15
00:00:21,198 --> 00:00:23,511
December, nineteen sixty-three.

16
00:00:23,511 --> 00:00:26,018
A seventeen-year-old named Randy Gardner

17
00:00:26,018 --> 00:00:27,333
had a wild idea for

18
00:00:27,333 --> 00:00:29,600
his school science fair.

19
00:00:29,701 --> 00:00:31,254
He would stay awake longer

20
00:00:31,254 --> 00:00:33,514
than any human ever recorded.

21
00:00:33,514 --> 00:00:34,779
No coffee.

22
00:00:34,779 --> 00:00:35,978
No pills.

23
00:00:35,978 --> 00:00:37,665
Just pure willpower.

24
00:00:37,766 --> 00:00:39,468
His friends watched him around

25
00:00:39,468 --> 00:00:40,181
the clock,

26
00:00:40,181 --> 00:00:41,883
and a Stanford sleep scientist

27
00:00:41,883 --> 00:00:43,879
showed up to take notes.

28
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:45,269
Eleven days later,

29
00:00:45,269 --> 00:00:46,701
Randy had set a record

30
00:00:46,701 --> 00:00:48,610
so extreme that Guinness World

31
00:00:48,610 --> 00:00:51,414
Records eventually stopped accepting attempts

32
00:00:51,414 --> 00:00:52,804
to beat it.

33
00:00:53,406 --> 00:00:53,993
So today,

34
00:00:53,993 --> 00:00:55,824
you're going to try it too.

35
00:00:55,874 --> 00:00:57,304
Eleven days?

36
00:00:57,304 --> 00:00:58,415
You can't even stay up

37
00:00:58,415 --> 00:00:59,660
past midnight.

38
00:00:59,710 --> 00:01:00,740
Watch me.

39
00:01:00,790 --> 00:01:02,037
Your friend Max is in

40
00:01:02,037 --> 00:01:04,201
charge of keeping you awake.

41
00:01:04,201 --> 00:01:05,813
The rules are simple.

42
00:01:05,813 --> 00:01:06,969
No naps.

43
00:01:06,969 --> 00:01:08,563
No microsleeps.

44
00:01:08,563 --> 00:01:09,602
Not even a blink that

45
00:01:09,602 --> 00:01:10,934
lasts too long.

46
00:01:11,535 --> 00:01:12,894
Hour sixteen.

47
00:01:12,894 --> 00:01:14,010
It's two in the morning,

48
00:01:14,010 --> 00:01:15,091
and honestly?

49
00:01:15,091 --> 00:01:16,537
You feel fine.

50
00:01:17,425 --> 00:01:19,004
Maybe a little wired.

51
00:01:19,004 --> 00:01:20,724
Your brain makes a chemical

52
00:01:20,724 --> 00:01:21,934
called adenosine,

53
00:01:21,934 --> 00:01:23,417
and every hour you stay

54
00:01:23,417 --> 00:01:25,786
awake, more of it builds up.

55
00:01:25,887 --> 00:01:26,661
Your body,

56
00:01:26,661 --> 00:01:28,565
however, knows exactly what time

57
00:01:28,565 --> 00:01:29,543
it is.

58
00:01:29,543 --> 00:01:31,341
Your temperature is dropping,

59
00:01:31,341 --> 00:01:32,876
and your internal clock is

60
00:01:32,876 --> 00:01:34,631
flooding you with signals that

61
00:01:34,631 --> 00:01:36,190
scream: it's night.

62
00:01:36,190 --> 00:01:37,463
Go to bed.

63
00:01:38,065 --> 00:01:39,653
Think of adenosine like sand

64
00:01:39,653 --> 00:01:41,509
piling up in your head.

65
00:01:41,509 --> 00:01:43,544
Sleep is what sweeps it away.

66
00:01:43,544 --> 00:01:45,518
Tonight, nobody is sweeping.

67
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:47,408
Coffee would help.

68
00:01:47,408 --> 00:01:49,492
Caffeine blocks adenosine from docking

69
00:01:49,492 --> 00:01:50,834
in your brain.

70
00:01:50,834 --> 00:01:52,753
But it doesn't remove the sand.

71
00:01:52,753 --> 00:01:54,100
It just hides it.

72
00:01:54,150 --> 00:01:55,847
Put the coffee down.

73
00:01:55,847 --> 00:01:57,611
Rules are rules.

74
00:01:58,213 --> 00:01:59,658
Hour twenty-four.

75
00:01:59,658 --> 00:02:01,071
One full day.

76
00:02:01,959 --> 00:02:02,826
In the morning,

77
00:02:02,826 --> 00:02:03,990
you decide to drive out

78
00:02:03,990 --> 00:02:05,214
for breakfast.

79
00:02:05,214 --> 00:02:06,462
Bad idea.

80
00:02:06,563 --> 00:02:08,141
Here's something wild.

81
00:02:08,141 --> 00:02:10,022
After twenty-four hours awake,

82
00:02:10,022 --> 00:02:11,685
your reactions are about as

83
00:02:11,685 --> 00:02:13,061
slow as someone who is

84
00:02:13,061 --> 00:02:14,574
legally drunk.

85
00:02:14,674 --> 00:02:16,048
And your brain starts doing

86
00:02:16,048 --> 00:02:17,618
something terrifying.

87
00:02:17,618 --> 00:02:19,097
Microsleeps.

88
00:02:19,198 --> 00:02:20,306
For a few seconds,

89
00:02:20,306 --> 00:02:21,728
parts of your brain simply

90
00:02:21,728 --> 00:02:22,881
switch off.

91
00:02:22,881 --> 00:02:24,563
Your eyes stay open.

92
00:02:24,563 --> 00:02:26,229
But nobody's home.

93
00:02:26,330 --> 00:02:27,579
At highway speed,

94
00:02:27,579 --> 00:02:29,944
a four-second microsleep means driving

95
00:02:29,944 --> 00:02:31,520
the length of a football

96
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:33,339
field with your eyes open,

97
00:02:33,339 --> 00:02:35,332
and your brain asleep.

98
00:02:35,382 --> 00:02:36,353
Hey!

99
00:02:36,353 --> 00:02:37,464
Wake up!

100
00:02:37,514 --> 00:02:38,388
Which is why,

101
00:02:38,388 --> 00:02:39,547
for the rest of this

102
00:02:39,547 --> 00:02:42,065
challenge, Max takes your car keys.

103
00:02:42,667 --> 00:02:43,790
Day two.

104
00:02:43,790 --> 00:02:45,715
Something is happening inside your

105
00:02:45,715 --> 00:02:47,996
head that you can't even feel.

106
00:02:48,884 --> 00:02:51,332
Scientists discovered that during deep

107
00:02:51,332 --> 00:02:53,229
sleep, gaps between your brain

108
00:02:53,229 --> 00:02:54,306
cells open up,

109
00:02:54,306 --> 00:02:56,068
and fluid rushes through,

110
00:02:56,068 --> 00:02:57,842
washing away the waste your

111
00:02:57,842 --> 00:02:59,760
brain made all day.

112
00:02:59,861 --> 00:03:01,669
One of those waste products

113
00:03:01,669 --> 00:03:03,415
is a sticky protein called

114
00:03:03,415 --> 00:03:04,437
beta amyloid,

115
00:03:04,437 --> 00:03:06,059
the same stuff linked to

116
00:03:06,059 --> 00:03:07,980
Alzheimer's disease.

117
00:03:08,081 --> 00:03:09,049
In one study,

118
00:03:09,049 --> 00:03:10,798
a single night without sleep

119
00:03:10,798 --> 00:03:12,198
was enough to raise it

120
00:03:12,198 --> 00:03:13,894
in people's brains.

121
00:03:13,894 --> 00:03:15,548
You've now skipped two.

122
00:03:16,149 --> 00:03:17,259
Randy, by the way,

123
00:03:17,259 --> 00:03:19,723
was already struggling by day two.

124
00:03:19,723 --> 00:03:21,411
He got moody and clumsy,

125
00:03:21,411 --> 00:03:23,158
and he had trouble focusing

126
00:03:23,158 --> 00:03:23,906
his eyes,

127
00:03:23,906 --> 00:03:25,051
so he had to give

128
00:03:25,051 --> 00:03:26,692
up watching TV.

129
00:03:27,294 --> 00:03:29,819
Meanwhile, your stomach has gone rogue.

130
00:03:29,819 --> 00:03:31,306
Your body pumps out more

131
00:03:31,306 --> 00:03:33,069
ghrelin, the hunger hormone,

132
00:03:33,069 --> 00:03:34,190
and less leptin,

133
00:03:34,190 --> 00:03:35,792
the hormone that tells you

134
00:03:35,792 --> 00:03:37,130
you're full.

135
00:03:37,231 --> 00:03:38,247
So at four in the

136
00:03:38,247 --> 00:03:40,011
morning, you're standing in front

137
00:03:40,011 --> 00:03:40,952
of the fridge,

138
00:03:40,952 --> 00:03:42,107
eating cold pizza,

139
00:03:42,107 --> 00:03:43,604
and you don't even remember

140
00:03:43,604 --> 00:03:44,896
walking here.

141
00:03:44,946 --> 00:03:45,930
Dude.

142
00:03:45,930 --> 00:03:47,704
That was my pizza.

143
00:03:48,305 --> 00:03:49,470
Day three.

144
00:03:49,470 --> 00:03:50,789
This is where things start

145
00:03:50,789 --> 00:03:52,065
to get weird.

146
00:03:52,953 --> 00:03:54,884
Your emotions go haywire.

147
00:03:54,884 --> 00:03:56,936
Researchers found that the brain's

148
00:03:56,936 --> 00:03:57,839
fear center,

149
00:03:57,839 --> 00:03:58,800
the amygdala,

150
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:00,735
became about sixty percent more

151
00:04:00,735 --> 00:04:03,575
reactive after a night without sleep.

152
00:04:03,625 --> 00:04:04,576
Hey.

153
00:04:04,576 --> 00:04:06,140
Want some toast?

154
00:04:06,190 --> 00:04:07,002
Why would you SAY that

155
00:04:07,002 --> 00:04:07,695
to me?!

156
00:04:07,745 --> 00:04:08,837
You cry.

157
00:04:08,837 --> 00:04:10,149
Then you laugh.

158
00:04:10,149 --> 00:04:11,631
Then you're furious.

159
00:04:11,631 --> 00:04:12,785
Then you forget what you

160
00:04:12,785 --> 00:04:14,277
were furious about.

161
00:04:14,879 --> 00:04:16,254
To see what's going on,

162
00:04:16,254 --> 00:04:18,043
you visit a sleep lab.

163
00:04:18,093 --> 00:04:19,520
Your brain waves look half

164
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:20,947
asleep, even with your eyes

165
00:04:20,947 --> 00:04:22,040
wide open.

166
00:04:22,090 --> 00:04:23,908
Your words start slurring.

167
00:04:23,908 --> 00:04:26,108
Simple math feels impossible.

168
00:04:26,108 --> 00:04:27,494
Randy was asked to count

169
00:04:27,494 --> 00:04:30,118
backwards from one hundred by sevens.

170
00:04:30,118 --> 00:04:31,230
He got to sixty-five,

171
00:04:31,230 --> 00:04:32,594
and just stopped.

172
00:04:32,644 --> 00:04:34,090
Why did you stop?

173
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:35,104
Stop...

174
00:04:35,104 --> 00:04:35,911
what?

175
00:04:35,961 --> 00:04:37,113
He said he had forgotten

176
00:04:37,113 --> 00:04:38,568
what he was doing.

177
00:04:39,170 --> 00:04:40,304
Day four.

178
00:04:40,304 --> 00:04:42,515
Remember the figure in the corner?

179
00:04:43,404 --> 00:04:44,573
Your brain is now so

180
00:04:44,573 --> 00:04:45,827
desperate for sleep,

181
00:04:45,827 --> 00:04:47,527
it starts dreaming while you're

182
00:04:47,527 --> 00:04:48,852
still awake.

183
00:04:48,953 --> 00:04:50,722
The walls seem to breathe.

184
00:04:50,722 --> 00:04:52,783
Patterns crawl across the floor.

185
00:04:52,783 --> 00:04:53,908
Shadows move at the edge

186
00:04:53,908 --> 00:04:55,199
of your vision.

187
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:57,250
Randy hallucinated too.

188
00:04:57,250 --> 00:04:58,679
At one point he mistook

189
00:04:58,679 --> 00:04:59,880
a street sign for a

190
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:01,709
person, and he became convinced

191
00:05:01,709 --> 00:05:04,294
he was a famous football player.

192
00:05:04,895 --> 00:05:06,737
Then paranoia creeps in.

193
00:05:06,737 --> 00:05:08,012
You start to think Max

194
00:05:08,012 --> 00:05:10,339
is secretly working against you.

195
00:05:10,389 --> 00:05:11,326
Uh...

196
00:05:11,326 --> 00:05:12,303
why are you staring at

197
00:05:12,303 --> 00:05:13,460
me like that?

198
00:05:14,062 --> 00:05:15,267
Day five.

199
00:05:15,267 --> 00:05:17,331
You can't remember yesterday.

200
00:05:17,331 --> 00:05:18,457
Literally.

201
00:05:19,345 --> 00:05:20,942
Deep inside your brain,

202
00:05:20,942 --> 00:05:22,729
the hippocampus works like a

203
00:05:22,729 --> 00:05:24,145
memory inbox.

204
00:05:24,145 --> 00:05:25,327
Sleep is when it saves

205
00:05:25,327 --> 00:05:27,204
the day's files for good.

206
00:05:27,305 --> 00:05:28,276
Without sleep,

207
00:05:28,276 --> 00:05:30,026
the inbox overflows.

208
00:05:30,026 --> 00:05:31,007
In one study,

209
00:05:31,007 --> 00:05:33,312
sleep-deprived people were about forty

210
00:05:33,312 --> 00:05:36,325
percent worse at forming new memories.

211
00:05:36,926 --> 00:05:37,715
By day six,

212
00:05:37,715 --> 00:05:39,762
Max is the one struggling.

213
00:05:39,762 --> 00:05:41,280
It turns out babysitting a

214
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:43,366
sleep zombie is exhausting.

215
00:05:43,416 --> 00:05:44,205
Hey.

216
00:05:44,205 --> 00:05:45,004
Hey!

217
00:05:45,004 --> 00:05:46,168
No sleeping.

218
00:05:46,168 --> 00:05:47,481
Rules are rules.

219
00:05:48,083 --> 00:05:49,282
Day seven.

220
00:05:49,282 --> 00:05:50,955
You're past the halfway point,

221
00:05:50,955 --> 00:05:52,325
and your body is starting

222
00:05:52,325 --> 00:05:53,719
to fall apart.

223
00:05:54,607 --> 00:05:56,661
Your immune defenses weaken.

224
00:05:56,661 --> 00:05:58,600
Your blood sugar control slips

225
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:01,781
toward levels doctors see in pre-diabetes.

226
00:06:01,781 --> 00:06:02,879
And you feel cold all

227
00:06:02,879 --> 00:06:03,967
the time.

228
00:06:04,068 --> 00:06:05,877
And here's the darkest part.

229
00:06:05,877 --> 00:06:07,299
In the nineteen-eighties,

230
00:06:07,299 --> 00:06:10,205
scientists kept rats awake without stopping.

231
00:06:10,205 --> 00:06:11,359
Within a few weeks,

232
00:06:11,359 --> 00:06:13,591
every single one of them died.

233
00:06:13,692 --> 00:06:15,638
Nobody knows exactly where the

234
00:06:15,638 --> 00:06:16,769
human limit is,

235
00:06:16,769 --> 00:06:18,776
because no scientist would ever

236
00:06:18,776 --> 00:06:20,745
let anyone find out.

237
00:06:20,846 --> 00:06:22,562
There's even a rare genetic

238
00:06:22,562 --> 00:06:25,198
disease called fatal familial insomnia,

239
00:06:25,198 --> 00:06:27,037
where people slowly lose the

240
00:06:27,037 --> 00:06:29,276
ability to sleep at all.

241
00:06:29,878 --> 00:06:30,981
Day ten.

242
00:06:30,981 --> 00:06:32,625
Your face looks blank.

243
00:06:32,625 --> 00:06:34,460
Your eyes won't focus.

244
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:36,428
Your voice is slow and flat.

245
00:06:37,316 --> 00:06:37,819
And yet,

246
00:06:37,819 --> 00:06:39,533
something strange happens.

247
00:06:39,533 --> 00:06:41,470
Some parts of you still work.

248
00:06:41,470 --> 00:06:42,738
On his final night,

249
00:06:42,738 --> 00:06:44,518
Randy beat the sleep scientist

250
00:06:44,518 --> 00:06:45,751
at pinball.

251
00:06:45,751 --> 00:06:47,526
Over and over again.

252
00:06:48,128 --> 00:06:50,298
Your brain is quietly cheating.

253
00:06:50,298 --> 00:06:51,979
Tiny groups of neurons take

254
00:06:51,979 --> 00:06:53,232
turns nodding off,

255
00:06:53,232 --> 00:06:54,797
like a city switching off

256
00:06:54,797 --> 00:06:56,705
one light at a time.

257
00:06:57,307 --> 00:06:59,408
Hour two hundred sixty-four.

258
00:06:59,408 --> 00:07:00,718
Eleven days.

259
00:07:00,718 --> 00:07:01,830
You did it.

260
00:07:01,830 --> 00:07:03,363
You tied the record.

261
00:07:04,251 --> 00:07:05,447
No way.

262
00:07:05,447 --> 00:07:07,164
You actually did it!

263
00:07:07,766 --> 00:07:08,850
And then...

264
00:07:08,850 --> 00:07:10,084
you sleep.

265
00:07:10,185 --> 00:07:12,746
Randy slept for about fourteen hours.

266
00:07:12,746 --> 00:07:13,758
When he woke up,

267
00:07:13,758 --> 00:07:15,896
he felt mostly normal again.

268
00:07:15,997 --> 00:07:18,871
Doctors found no serious lasting damage.

269
00:07:18,871 --> 00:07:20,150
But decades later,

270
00:07:20,150 --> 00:07:22,224
Randy told reporters he struggled

271
00:07:22,224 --> 00:07:24,390
with insomnia for years.

272
00:07:24,491 --> 00:07:25,739
Your brain is amazing at

273
00:07:25,739 --> 00:07:26,894
catching up.

274
00:07:26,894 --> 00:07:28,198
It doesn't pay back every

275
00:07:28,198 --> 00:07:29,291
lost hour.

276
00:07:29,291 --> 00:07:31,030
Instead, it dives into extra

277
00:07:31,030 --> 00:07:33,069
deep sleep and extra dreaming,

278
00:07:33,069 --> 00:07:35,508
to repair what matters most.

279
00:07:35,609 --> 00:07:37,352
So that's the happy ending.

280
00:07:37,352 --> 00:07:38,368
Right?

281
00:07:38,469 --> 00:07:39,340
Well...

282
00:07:39,340 --> 00:07:40,778
here's the twist.

283
00:07:41,380 --> 00:07:42,746
Almost no one will ever

284
00:07:42,746 --> 00:07:44,869
stay awake for eleven days.

285
00:07:44,869 --> 00:07:46,170
But about one in three

286
00:07:46,170 --> 00:07:48,066
adults regularly sleeps less than

287
00:07:48,066 --> 00:07:49,864
seven hours a night.

288
00:07:49,965 --> 00:07:51,234
In one experiment,

289
00:07:51,234 --> 00:07:52,821
two weeks of sleeping six

290
00:07:52,821 --> 00:07:54,466
hours a night hurt people's

291
00:07:54,466 --> 00:07:56,052
focus as badly as staying

292
00:07:56,052 --> 00:07:58,311
up for two nights straight.

293
00:07:58,311 --> 00:07:59,824
And the scariest part?

294
00:07:59,824 --> 00:08:01,952
They only felt a little sleepy.

295
00:08:02,554 --> 00:08:04,233
Which means the most dangerous

296
00:08:04,233 --> 00:08:06,017
version of this experiment might

297
00:08:06,017 --> 00:08:07,381
be the one you're running

298
00:08:07,381 --> 00:08:08,589
right now.

299
00:08:08,690 --> 00:08:09,644
So...

